      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Hardy vs tender: this entry is the hardy F. magellanica (UK in the ground, pots in Spain's shade); most shop fuchsias are tender hybrids (RHS tender guide: H1C to H3 by cultivar, frost-free overwintering, standards need winter protection). Watering: RHS tender guide says compost moist but not soggy, containers and baskets roughly daily, easy to overwater new pots; the hardy guide gives no pot frequency; NC State says cool and moist. Interval 2 days is a guess. Light: RHS best in full sun but happy in dappled or partial shade, no flowers in deep shade; NC State shade-loving but takes full sun, flowering declines in summer heat; stored bright_indirect as the gentler reading (rule 5). Size: NC State 5 to 10 ft by 5 to 10 ft (upper value 300 cm); RHS gives no figure and says most hardy fuchsias are not overly large; from_knowledge_verify. Growth medium (NC State). Hardiness: RHS hardy guide gives no H rating or temperature; stems die to the ground in cold areas and regrow; NC State USDA zones 5a to 10b; no temperature stored. Toxicity: no ASPCA entry for F. magellanica was found (the ASPCA's honeysuckle fuchsia is a different plant); NC State carries non-toxic tags for cats and dogs with no text, so 0 is from_knowledge_verify and weak: listed under pets unknown for review. Berries: RHS says all fuchsia fruit is edible, NC State says not edible by humans (not stated as poisonous); no human exposure stored. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
